46
00:03:43,781 --> 00:03:47,513
But if you recall
the picture that we said,

47
00:03:47,513 --> 00:03:52,799
the use case three set up on your own,
we said GRE tunneling,

48
00:03:52,799 --> 00:03:56,646
that would be another viable alternative.

49
00:03:56,646 --> 00:04:00,843
But you could see great,
all of this networking inside of Linux,

50
00:04:00,843 --> 00:04:02,837
that's what flannel sets up.

51
00:04:02,837 --> 00:04:05,729
And so now through what it's set up,

52
00:04:05,729 --> 00:04:10,956
a container inside of this flannel
network could have an address,

53
00:04:10,956 --> 00:04:16,387
10.244.0.1, maybe this one is 10.244.1.1.

54
00:04:16,387 --> 00:04:21,323
And they can communicate with
each other through the veth,

55
00:04:21,323 --> 00:04:24,983
through the bridge, through this tunnel.

56
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:31,113
The vxlan device through the physical
node, go across a physical network,

57
00:04:31,113 --> 00:04:35,250
come into here and
then do the reverse, all right?

58
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:41,324
So that's flannel and
that's what kind of a network plugin does.

59
00:04:41,324 --> 00:04:48,283
Now the next thing is how do pod
to multiple pods communicate?

60
00:04:48,283 --> 00:04:53,071
So we talked about last time,
but kind of reiterating here,

61
00:04:53,071 --> 00:04:58,454
the contract in Kubernetes is
called a service to represent this.

62
00:04:58,454 --> 00:05:04,117
So a service is an abstraction which
defines a local logical set of pods and

63
00:05:04,117 --> 00:05:06,686
a policy by which to access them.

64
00:05:06,686 --> 00:05:09,907
And then it load balances between them,

65
00:05:09,907 --> 00:05:14,654
so it uses a selector to see
which nodes should be in its set.

66
00:05:14,654 --> 00:05:18,144
All right, so we know that we saw
the YAML files, we deployed it,

67
00:05:18,144 --> 00:05:21,082
it's in the GitHub repo,
you can try it yourself.

68
00:05:21,082 --> 00:05:23,216
So how is this implemented?

69
00:05:23,216 --> 00:05:27,320
Is with a component called kube-proxy.

70
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:32,428
So Kube-proxy is a component shown
right here, that makes sure clients

71
00:05:32,428 --> 00:05:37,296
can connect to the services,
you define load balanced when needed.

72
00:05:37,296 --> 00:05:39,207
It runs on every node and

73
00:05:39,207 --> 00:05:44,472
now it's not an actual proxy in
that it's in the path of traffic.

74
00:05:44,472 --> 00:05:48,257
It's actually not in the path of traffic,

75
00:05:48,257 --> 00:05:52,262
it's just an interface to iptables or
ipvs.

76
00:05:52,262 --> 00:05:57,241
All right, so the API server,
when it changes something

77
00:05:57,241 --> 00:06:01,805
like maybe the endpoints
that are part of a service,

78
00:06:01,805 --> 00:06:05,038
great, kube-proxy will see that.

79
00:06:05,038 --> 00:06:09,783
And since it's running on each node,
it will update the iptables or

80
00:06:09,783 --> 00:06:11,875
ipvs running in Kubernetes.

81
00:06:11,875 --> 00:06:18,356
So when you set up your cluster, you can
say which one you want to actually use.

82
00:06:18,356 --> 00:06:23,172
So I believe iptables is the default,

83
00:06:23,172 --> 00:06:27,240
but you can also say to use ipvs.

84
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:33,912
So now when a client talks to a pod,
it's actually going through iptables or

85
00:06:33,912 --> 00:06:38,619
ipvs to load balance between those pods,
all right?

86
00:06:38,619 --> 00:06:43,923
So we clearly saw how to do ipvs,
it does load balancing.

87
00:06:43,923 --> 00:06:47,360
But how do you do load
balancing with iptables?

88
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,563
So luckily there's this nice
site that had a really nice

89
00:06:52,563 --> 00:06:57,992
example of how you could do
load balancing with iptables.

90
00:06:57,992 --> 00:07:01,918
So you can use this module
called statistic and

91
00:07:01,918 --> 00:07:08,571
then basically say with probability
match this particular rule, all right?

92
00:07:08,571 --> 00:07:12,788
So you're going to match
all this other stuff, so

93
00:07:12,788 --> 00:07:16,383
this is the nat table prerouting chain.

94
00:07:16,383 --> 00:07:20,144
Match this destination IP address,

95
00:07:20,144 --> 00:07:26,828
match this destination port, and
with probability match this.

96
00:07:26,828 --> 00:07:32,118
So if it matches that with 33%
probability it'll match this and

97
00:07:32,118 --> 00:07:36,373
then it'll choose destination 10.0.0.2.

98
00:07:36,373 --> 00:07:40,052
And the next one with 50% probability,
choose that and

99
00:07:40,052 --> 00:07:43,145
then the last one is
with 100% probability.

100
00:07:43,145 --> 00:07:48,136
So why it's kind of 33 5100 percent

101
00:07:48,136 --> 00:07:52,685
is because if it didn't match this,

102
00:07:52,685 --> 00:07:57,821
now you need to select between these two,

103
00:07:57,821 --> 00:08:03,715
so that gives each a 33%
chance of matching.

104
00:08:03,715 --> 00:08:07,228
[COUGH] So
kind of walking through an example,

105
00:08:07,228 --> 00:08:11,951
let's say we mentioned that
the service gets an IP address.

106
00:08:11,951 --> 00:08:16,045
No interfaces are created,
just it's a virtual IP address and

107
00:08:16,045 --> 00:08:18,911
then let's say we have example endpoints.

108
00:08:18,911 --> 00:08:24,383
10.244.1.2, that's over here on node 1 and

109
00:08:24,383 --> 00:08:29,286
2.2 on node 2, so
that's this one right here.

110
00:08:29,286 --> 00:08:32,570
So let's say it's zero wants
to talk to the service.

111
00:08:32,570 --> 00:08:38,852
It comes into the source, and destination
is the source of this container,

112
00:08:38,852 --> 00:08:42,894
destination is the destination
of the service.

113
00:08:42,894 --> 00:08:47,223
It'll come into the iptables node and

